{"id":"emergency-spending-by-year","title":"Spending under emergency powers, year by year","question":"How much has each department paid under emergency purchasing rules since fiscal 2019, and has it stopped?","finding":"The City paid $1.51 billion under emergency purchasing rules from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date: $65,177 in fiscal 2019, $443.3 million at the peak in fiscal 2021, and still $198.7 million in fiscal 2024, $100.8 million in 2025 and $71.9 million in 2026. Homelessness Services paid under the wording \"grants only, no bid, emergency procurement\" in every year from fiscal 2020 to fiscal 2027 ($726.9 million in all, $305.9 million of it since July 2023). Emergency public-works contracts, almost unused before fiscal 2024, have drawn $62.6 million since.","how":"Payments in the Controller’s vendor-payment file whose purchasing rule contains the word \"emergency\", added up by department and by the fiscal year (July to June) in which each was issued, which is read from its voucher number and not from the year the file lists it under. Fiscal 2027 runs from July 2026 to the retrieval date. A year counts as a year of use when the department paid $10,000 or more under such a rule.","limits":"On emergency purchasing: Administrative Code chapters 21 and 6 let a department buy without bids when a sudden danger leaves no time, and the pandemic was such a time. The record does not say which emergency a payment relies on. A payment in a later year can be the last invoice on work done earlier, and the grant wording may be a label that stayed on a contract after the emergency that justified it had ended. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days. Payments that belong to fiscal 2018 or earlier are in none of the figures here. None of them was keyed under an emergency rule.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid under emergency rules, fiscal 2019–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"first","t":"First year","type":"year"},{"k":"last","t":"Latest year","type":"year"},{"k":"years","t":"Years of use","type":"int"},{"k":"peakYear","t":"Peak year","type":"year"},{"k":"peak","t":"Paid in peak year","type":"usd"},{"k":"fy2026","t":"Paid in fiscal 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"fy2027","t":"Fiscal 2027 to date","type":"usd"},{"k":"topVendor","t":"Supplier paid most","type":"org"},{"k":"topVendorPaid","t":"Paid to that supplier","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"department":"Homelessness Services","paid":747709682,"first":2020,"last":2027,"years":8,"peakYear":2023,"peak":211810150,"fy2026":35096912,"fy2027":6787528,"topVendorPaid":178927469,"topVendor":"Tenderloin Housing Clinic Inc","topVendorKey":"TENDERLOIN HOUSING CLINIC"},{"department":"Human Services Agency","paid":388019817,"first":2020,"last":2026,"years":5,"peakYear":2021,"peak":224371304,"fy2026":9100000,"fy2027":0,"topVendorPaid":62757735,"topVendor":"Hotel Whitcomb","topVendorKey":null},{"department":"Public Health","paid":264532980,"first":2020,"last":2026,"years":7,"peakYear":2021,"peak":130569148,"fy2026":727967,"fy2027":0,"topVendorPaid":48545230,"topVendor":"Color Health Inc.","topVendorKey":null},{"department":"Public Works","paid":64268025,"first":2019,"last":2027,"years":9,"peakYear":2026,"peak":26298795,"fy2026":26298795,"fy2027":10531567,"topVendorPaid":29012967,"topVendor":"Clark Construction Group - Calif LP","topVendorKey":null},{"department":"Economic & Workforce Development","paid":14502670